holy:ZEROe dcterms:references <https://www.airbus.com/en/innovation/zero-emission/hydrogen/zeroe>;
           holy:relatesToApplication holy:ZEROeTurboprop,
                                     holy:ZEROeBlendedWingBody,
                                     holy:ZEROeTurbofan;
           m4i:endOfProject "2035"@en;
           m4i:projectParticipant resource:Airbus;
           m4i:startOfProject "2020"@en;
           a holy:ProductDevelopmentProject,
             owl:NamedIndividual;
           rdfs:label "ZEROe"@en;
           skos:definition """An project from Airbus to develop the world's first zero-emission commercial aircraft by 2035. It is based on hydrogen propulsion. Airbus is working on three aircraft prototypes.

All three ZEROe concepts are hybrid-hydrogen aircraft. They are powered by hydrogen combustion through modified gas turbine engines. Liquid hydrogen is used as fuel for combustion with oxygen.

In addition, hydrogen fuel cells create electrical power that complements the gas turbine, resulting in a highly efficient hybrid-electric propulsion system. All of these technologies are complementary, and the benefits are additive.

In 2022, we launched our ZEROe demonstrator with the aim to test hydrogen combustion technology on an A380 multimodal platform. A mature technology readiness level for a hydrogen-combustion propulsion system is expected to be achieved by 2025."""@en.
holy:ZEROeBlendedWingBody dcterms:references <https://www.airbus.com/en/innovation/zero-emission/hydrogen/zeroe>;
                          holy:relatesToApplication holy:ZEROe;
                          holy:usesProduct resource:Hydrogen_Tank,
                                           resource:Hydrogen_engine;
                          a owl:NamedIndividual,
                            holy:Aircraft;
                          rdfs:label "ZEROe Blended-Wing Body"@en;
                          skos:definition """Concept passenger aircraft. The exceptionally wide interior opens up multiple options for hydrogen storage and distribution. Here, the liquid hydrogen storage tanks are stored underneath the wings. Two hybrid-hydrogen turbofan engines provide thrust.

Range: 2000nm
Passengers: <200"""@en.
holy:ZEROeTurbofan dcterms:references <https://www.airbus.com/en/innovation/zero-emission/hydrogen/zeroe>;
                   holy:relatesToApplication holy:ZEROe;
                   a owl:NamedIndividual,
                     holy:Aircraft;
                   rdfs:label "ZEROe Turbofan"@en;
                   skos:definition """Passenger Aircraft running on two hybrid-hydrogen turbofan engines providing thrust. The liquid hydrogen storage and distribution system is located behind the rear pressure bulkhead.

Range: +1000 nm
Passengers: <100"""@en.
holy:ZEROeTurboprop dcterms:references <https://www.airbus.com/en/innovation/zero-emission/hydrogen/zeroe>;
                    holy:relatesToApplication holy:ZEROe;
                    a owl:NamedIndividual,
                      holy:Aircraft;
                    rdfs:label "ZEROe Turboprop"@en;
                    skos:definition """Passenger aircraft with two hybrid-hydrogen turboprop engines, which drive eight-bladed propellers, providing thrust. The liquid hydrogen storage and distribution system is located behind the rear pressure bulkhead.

Range: 2000 nm
Passengers: <200"""@en.
